229.	Management of the Hot Swollen Joint
The hot swollen joint is a common presentation and has extensive 
differential diagnosis. Some of the most serious causes are septic arthritis 
(with a case fatality of 11%) and acute leukemia in children. Late diagnosis 
and poor treatment leads to joint damage and death.  
The commonest affected joint is the big toe, typically due to gout 
and can be diagnosed on clinical grounds. Patients with a short history of 
a hot, swollen and tender joint (or joints) with restriction of movement 
should be regarded as having septic arthritis until proven otherwise. If 
clinical suspicion is high, then it is imperative to treat as septic arthritis 
even in the absence of fever.
