st/orthopaedic surgeon. 232. Systemic lupus erythematosus Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) is a chronic multisystem autoimmune inflammatory disease that has variable signs and follows a relapsing and remitting course. More than 90% of cases of SLE occur in women, commonly of childbearing age. This condition can lead to complications such as acute or chronic renal failure, seizures, psychosis, pleurisy, pleural effusion, pneumonitis, pulmonary hypertension, pericarditis, myocarditis, leukopaenia, lymphopaenia, thrombocytopaenia and interstitial lung disease. Management of SLE often depends on the individual patient’s disease severity and disease manifestations. Causes yy Autoimmune yy Genetic and environmental factors Symptoms yy Fatigue yy Malaise yy Fever yy Weight loss yy Rash yy Hair loss yy Mouth sores yy Joint pains yy Sensitivity to sunlight yy Seizures yy Nausea yy Dyspepsia yy Abdominal pain Signs yy Lymphadenopathy yy Fever yy Malar rash yy Discoid rash yy Fatigue yy Arthralgia yy Arthropathy yy Myalgia yy Arthritis yy Photosensitivity rash','— Systemic lupus erythematosus —
