232.	Systemic lupus erythematosus
Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) is a chronic multisystem 
autoimmune inflammatory disease that has variable signs and follows a 
relapsing and remitting course. More than 90% of cases of SLE occur in 
women, commonly of childbearing age.
This condition can lead to complications such as acute or chronic 
renal failure, seizures, psychosis, pleurisy, pleural effusion, pneumonitis, 
pulmonary 
hypertension, 
pericarditis, 
myocarditis, 
leukopaenia, 
lymphopaenia, thrombocytopaenia and interstitial lung disease.
Management of SLE often depends on the individual patient’s 
disease severity and disease manifestations. 
