('247','Osteoporosis','6','Problems of the Newborn (Neonate)  .......................................','619','619-619','yy Back pain yy Inability to walk after a very trivial fall','yy Deformed limbs yy Fractures yy Stooping stance','yy X-rays of wrists/hips yy Bone Mineral Density (BMD) scan yy Quantitative CT scan','Treatment objectives yy Management of underlying risk factors yy Prevention of fractures yy Early treatment of fractures Non-pharmacological treatment yy Dietary (adequate intake of calcium and vitamin-D containing foods) yy Therapeutic Lifestyle Changes (refer to risk factors in the preamble above) Pharmacological treatment A. For low fracture risk 1st Line Treatment Evidence Rating: [A] yy Calcium with Vitamin-D, oral, Adults',NULL,'— Osteoporosis —
Chapter 23: Trauma And Injuries
619
247.	Osteoporosis
Osteoporosis is a systemic skeletal disease characterized by low bone 
mass and small areas of deterioration of bone tissue, with a consequent 
increase in bone fragility and susceptibility to fracture. Risk of fractures is 
higher in elderly individuals with poor walking balance and vision.
Some risk factors include parental hip fractures, advanced 
age - especially women after menopause, smoking, alcohol abuse, 
immobilization, inadequate intake of vitamin-D and calcium, prolonged 
steroid use, hyperthyroidism, hyperparathyroidism, type-I diabetes 
mellitus, rheumatoid arthritis, malabsorption syndromes, and low femoral 
neck Bone Mineral Density (BMD). 
Cause 
yy
Imbalance between new bone formation and old bone breakdown 
yy
Bone mineral loss
yy
Low bone density 
Symptoms
yy
Back pain
yy
Inability to walk after a very trivial fall
Signs
yy
Deformed limbs 
yy
Fractures
yy
Stooping stance
Investigations
yy
X-rays of wrists/hips
yy
Bone Mineral Density (BMD) scan
yy
Quantitative CT scan
Treatment 
Treatment objectives
yy
Management of underlying risk factors
yy
Prevention of fractures
yy
Early treatment of fractures
Non-pharmacological treatment
yy
Dietary (adequate intake of calcium and vitamin-D containing foods) 
yy
Therapeutic Lifestyle Changes (refer to risk factors in the preamble 
above)
Pharmacological treatment 
A.      For low fracture risk
1st Line Treatment
Evidence Rating: [A]
yy
Calcium with Vitamin-D, oral,
Adults'),
