252.	Wounds
A wound is a break in the continuity of tissues in the body. It may 
involve overlying epithelium like skin. Wounds are usually caused by injury. 
Tissues affected include subcutaneous tissue, muscles and even bones. It 
may be small or large and may be deep or superficial. It may bleed, may 
be contaminated with dirt and other foreign matter and become infected. 
Causes 
yy
Mechanical agents e.g. cut from cutlass or knife, gunshot, accidents, 
contusion from blunt injury. Wounds may follow snake or insect 
bites, animal or human bites
yy
Chemical agents e.g. strong acids or alkalis, other corrosive chemicals
yy
Thermal injury resulting in burns
Symptoms
yy
Local pain
yy
Bleeding
yy
Discharge of pus if infected

— Wounds —
Chapter 23: Trauma And Injuries
631
Signs
yy
Local swelling and tenderness
yy
Look for other injuries e.g., head, chest, abdomen, bone, nerves
yy
Determine the physical characteristics of the wound e.g. site, size, 
shape and depth
Investigations
yy
Haemoglobin level if patient has bled
yy
Group and cross-match blood if indicated
yy
X-ray of injured part may be required to rule out osteomyelitis and 
bone fractures
yy
Wound swab for culture and sensitivity if wound is infected
Treatment  
Treatment objectives
yy
To control bleeding
yy
To relieve pain 
yy
To prevent or treat infection
yy
To protect against tetanus
yy
To promote wound healing 
Non-pharmacological treatment
yy
Apply sterile pressure dressing to bleeding site and raise the injured 
part to control bleeding.
yy
 If a bleeding vessel can be identified, it should be ligated. (the use of 
tourniquet to stop bleeding is discouraged)
yy
Bleeding from a tooth socket - put a small piece of sterile gauze in the 
socket and ask the patient to bite on it.
Box 23-1:  Wound management
yy
Immediate closure of wounds is good, but this is not advisable if the wound 
is dirty or likely to become infected e.g. gunshot wounds, animal and human 
bites and wounds over 6 hours old. They should not be sewn up.
yy
Wash hands well and wear sterile gloves. Clean the wound with antiseptic 
solution. Scrub dirty wounds with antiseptic solution and irrigate with dilute 
hydrogen peroxide and saline.
yy
If there are bits of gravel, glass or dirt in the wound, remove them gently. Lift 
up all flaps of skin, clean under them, excise all dead tissue and cover the 
wound with sterile gauze. 
yy
Anaesthesia may be required.
yy
Do not use Eusol, which is both irritant and exposes patient to unnecessary 
borate levels Dress infected wound as often as needed with normal saline 
or povidone iodine lotion. Take wound swab for culture and sensitivity test 
if possible and start Amoxicillin (Amoxycillin) while waiting for results of 
wound culture
Pharmacological treatment 
1st Line Treatment
Evidence Rating: [C]'),
