256.	Antibiotic Prophylaxis in Surgery 
Antibiotic prophylaxis refers to the administration of antibiotics in 
patients to reduce the risk of perioperative sepsis. The main cause of 
morbidity and mortality in surgery is infectious complications.
Antibiotic prophylaxis is indicated in cases where sepsis is expected 
and could have disastrous local or generalised effects.
For prophylaxis to be effective, antibiotics must be given before 
contamination takes place or at the earliest possible time before infection 
is established. For surgery, therefore, it must be given IV about the time of 
induction of anaesthesia, so that tissues are saturated with the antibiotic 
before contamination occurs. 
Causes  
yy
Staphylococcus aureus
yy
Streptococcus spp.
yy
Enterobacteriaceae (GI)
yy
Anaerobes (GI)
yy
Coagulase negative staphylococci (especially cardiac surgery, and 
implantation surgery), etc.
Objectives
yy
Prevent infections 
yy
Prevent complications
Indications
Proven Indications
yy
Acute appendicitis and intestinal obstruction
yy
Surgery on the colon and rectum
yy
Surgery on the biliary tract
yy
Gastro-oesophageal and oro-pharyngeal surgery for carcinoma
yy
Hysterectomy
yy
Surgery in the presence of pus
yy
Patients with rheumatic heart disease
yy
Patients with congenital heart disease

