yy
Address airways and breathing before commencing external cardiac 
massage
yy
If cardiac output is not responding to CPR, reconsider the adequacy 
of airway and breathing support
SHOCK (INADEQUATE CIRCULATION)
Causes
yy
Severe gastroenteritis
yy
Diarrhoea
yy
Cardiac disease
Management
Resuscitation – every child in shock should have oxygen at a high flow 
rate. Venous or intraosseous access should be obtained and an infusion of 
normal saline at 20 ml/kg given
In drowsiness with sighing respirations check blood sugar and acid 
base balance.  Treat diabetic ketoacidosis with IV normal saline and insulin
In an unconscious child with pinpoint pupils consider opiate 
poisoning. Try naloxone.
Gastrointestinal: Usually presents with shock from fluid loss. 
Symptoms include vomiting, abdominal pain or bloody stools; signs may 
include abdominal tenderness or a mass. Consider surgical intervention
Give boluses of fluid to shocked children if first one not effective. 
Consider IV antibiotics in shocked children with no obvious fluid loss
